<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bayonet">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayonet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as plug bayonet, socket bayonet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A bayonet is a knife, dagger, sword or spike mounted on the barrel of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> or rifle, so that the gun can be used as a spear in close combat. The first recorded bayonet proper is the 'gun-knife' of the son-and-mother gun in the Chinese military treatise Binglu of 1606, a plug bayonet pushed into the barrel. Jean Martinet standardised plug bayonets for new French fusilier regiments in 1671; because they stopped the gun from firing, socket bayonets that fitted around the barrel replaced them, and by the mid-18th century most European armies had adopted these for massed infantry charges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayonet.jpg" alt="Bayonet" width="330" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auge=mit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US-Bajonette_verschiedener_Epochen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word bayonette dates from the 16th century, but it is not clear whether bayonets then were knives that could be fitted to a firearm or simply a type of knife. Cotgrave's dictionary of 1611 describes one as a small flat pocket dagger, and Pierre Borel wrote in 1655 of a long knife made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>. The derivation of the name from Bayonne is said to have no factual basis.</p><p>The first recorded bayonet proper is in the Chinese military treatise Binglu of 1606: a plug bayonet of about 57.6 cm for the breech-loading Son-and-mother gun, called a gun-knife and described as a short sword inserted into the barrel and secured by a slight twist. A soldier unable to reload in time was to attach it and hold the gun like a spear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The plug bayonet made it impossible to fire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a>, so soldiers had to fix it at the last moment before a melee. At Killiecrankie in 1689 Jacobite Highlanders fired one volley, dropped their muskets and overwhelmed the Scots Army with axes and swords before it could fix bayonets. Its leader, Hugh Mackay, is believed to have then introduced a socket bayonet of his own invention, with an offset blade fitting round the barrel so that the musket could be fired with the bayonet attached. Shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Peace of Ryswick</a> in 1697 the English and Germans abolished the pike and adopted socket bayonets.</p><p>The 18th century brought the sword bayonet, a long blade that doubled as a short sword, meant to let riflemen form a square against cavalry; the Baker Rifle is an early example. From 1865 the German states, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, issued sawback bayonets, which could also serve as tools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>From the 17th century the bayonet charge became the main infantry tactic into the early 20th century. Military writers already noted in the 19th century that most charges did not end in close combat, because one side usually fled first. In 18th-century battles fewer than 2% of wounds treated came from bayonets, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> under 1% of casualties, though charges often decided short engagements.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the charge across no man's land was the standard method early on but was rarely successful; British casualties on the first day of the Somme were 57,470, of whom 19,240 were killed. Opinion on long rifles and bayonets then reversed, and infantry turned to trench knives, grenades and entrenching tools. The Japanese made bayonet charges in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>, which Allied forces called Banzai charges.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayonet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their rising of 1820 forced Ferdinand I to grant the Spanish constitution of 1812 on 13 July, which he cancelled on 23 February 1821 before Austrian <b>bayonets</b>; repression then deepened.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On Hoover's orders, troops under Douglas MacArthur used tear gas and <b>bayonets</b> to clear camps of First World War veterans demanding early payment of bonuses.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1606</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The word bayonette is older, from the 16th century, but it is unclear whether it then named a knife fitted to a gun; its supposed origin in the town of Bayonne has no basis in fact.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket<span>by 1521</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=bayonet">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
